<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spurious Correlations</span> occur when a non-essential attribute is
correlated with the target attribute on the training distribution, and the inductive biases
of the model favor the spurious attribute. Such inductive biases often favor
“simpler” attributes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>,
such as background <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> or texture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. As a result,
spurious correlations are often referred to as “shortcut learning” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Spurious correlations create <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">biased</span> models, which can suffer performance degradation
<span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in-distribution</span> (when the combined “availability and predictivity” of the spurious attribute
are higher than that of the target attribute <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>),
under <span id="A1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">subpopulation shift</span> (when a minority group of bias-conflicting examples becomes more prominent in the test distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>),
or under <span id="A1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">domain shift</span> (when the new domain samples from a previously unseen distribution of bias-conflicting instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subpopulation Shift</span> is a type of distribution shift where small subpopulation (a “minority group”)
present on the training distribution becomes more frequent on the test distribution (these minority groups
often consist of correlation-conflicting examples). Some methods for handling subpopulation shift rely on
labeled access to subpopulation groups
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>,
while other approaches attempt to leverage the inherent <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">difficulty</span> of the minority group examples
to automatically identify and up-weight them
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Rather than inducing the subpopulation shift, methods for handling subpopulation shift are typically bench-marked by “worst-group” performance.
Note that while subpopulation shift often involved spurious correlations, other types of shifts are
also covered within the framework (including class and attribute imbalances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>).
For class imbalance, traditional methods like reweighting and up-sampling can be effective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>,
though more recent work focused on using label distribution aware losses to increase classification margins
on minority groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain Generalization</span> is the problem of generalizing to unseen domains using training data from multiple domains.
Approaches typically try to learn <span id="A1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">invariant</span> representations across domains,
such that the learned representations and predictor remain stable in novel domains
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>.
While most approaches rely on domain labels, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>
attempts to infer domain partitions. Similarly to subpopulation shift, domain generalization
often, but not always, involves spurious correlations. The distinction between
sup-population shift is not always clear (e.g. some works on domain generalization evaluate
methods on subpopulation shift benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>).
We consider generalizing from completely spuriously correlated data to be a particular
instance of domain generalization - and methods that use “internal” diversity metrics
to learn diverse or de-biased ensembles without target domain data
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain Adaptation</span> is a relaxed version of domain generalization which assumes
<span id="A1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unlabeled</span> access to the target domain. Early foundation work established
formal bounds on the target error as a function of the source error and divergence
between the source and target distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
This theoretical work motivated the development of methods that explicitly penalize
the distance between the source and target distributions, using maximum mean discrepancy
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> or correlation distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>.
Similarly motivated work proposes minimizing source-target discrepancy using adversarial training
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>,
learning representations that maximally confuse a discriminator trained to distinguish between
source and target domains.
While not central examples of domain adaptation, we consider ACE and other approaches
that use unlabeled target data to learn diverse ensembles
(DivDis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and D-BAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>),
to be a class of domain adaptation methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diverse Ensembles</span> were recognized as desirable for robust generalization early
in the development of modern machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
Modern deep ensembles typically use different initializations and dataset orderings to
produce diverse predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
However, ensembles often converge to similar local optima when the inductive biases of the model
strongly favor a given solution (e.g. using a particular spurious attribute). To promote
more diverse solutions, methods explicitly promote diversity by following different
eigenvectors of the hessian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, or adversarially
learning diverse latent representations
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>.
Most similar to ACE, DivDis and D-BAT explicitly penalize mutual information or agreement
in the predictions of the ensemble members
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>,
allowing them to handle extreme (in some cases complete) spurious correlations.
Diverse ensembles have also been used to improve scalable oversight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>,
acting as an uncertainty metric for active learning in reinforcement learning from human feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>
and mitigating over-optimization against reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Training</span> is an approach to semi-supervised learning which uses (hardened) model
predictions as training targets. Popular methods like pseudo-labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>
and entropy regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> rely on the <span id="A1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cluster assumption</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> - that data form clusters with each elements of each cluster sharing
the same class and classes separated by <span id="A1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">low density</span> regions.
Self-training methods have achieved state-of-the art performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>,
but pure pseudo-labeling approaches can cause instability and reinforce inaccurate predictions.
To overcome these issues, prior work suggests confidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and
uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> based thresholding,
as well as a gradual schedule for the pseudo-label loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
Some work has successfully applied self-training to domain-adaption
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
Most relevant to our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> proves that under certain conditions
self-training can improve robustness to spurious correlation (though they assume
the default classifier favors the ground-truth feature).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proper Scoring Under Different Mix Rates</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A scoring rule <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates how well a distribution <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> approximates <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. A scoring rule is <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proper</span> if the true distribution <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a global minimum: <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(q,q)\leq S(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(q,q)\leq S(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the context of diverse generalization, our true distribution <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is a joint distribution identical to the set of <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> concepts:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}|x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if}\ \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{C}(x)\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(\mathbf{y}|x)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if}\ \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{C}(x)\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{n}),\mathbf{C}(x)=(C_{1}(x),\dots,C_{F}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=(y_{1},\dots,y_{n}),\mathbf{C}(x)=(C_{1}(x),\dots,C_{F}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that our ensemble <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can similarly be expressed as a distribution over concepts, with <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{y}|x)=(p_{\theta_{1}}(y_{1}|x),\dots,p_{\theta_{F}}(y_{F}|x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\mathbf{y}|x)=(p_{\theta_{1}}(y_{1}|x),\dots,p_{\theta_{F}}(y_{F}|x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that (under complete search) proper scoring is <span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">necessary</span> but not <span id="A2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sufficient</span> for finding the true distribution. In particular, without strictly proper scoring (<math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(q,q)&lt;S(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(q,q)&lt;S(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q</annotation></semantics></math>), we may find global minima arbitrarily far from the true distribution. For example, vanilla ERM is (trivially) a proper scoring rule for diverse generalization, but insufficient for finding good approximations to <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. While stochastic gradient descent is not complete, in practice we expect losses that are proper scoring rules to be more effective.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the following subsections, we show that ACE is a proper scoring rule under all mix rates above the assumed lower bound. In contrast, we show DivDis and D-BAT are only proper under a unique mix rate. For simplicity, we only cover the case of <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For ease of notation, we let <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c_{i}}^{y}=p(C_{i}(x)=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c_{i}}^{y}=p(C_{i}(x)=y)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the marginal probability that the <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-th" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-th</annotation></semantics></math> concept equals <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full marginal distribution, and <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c_{1}c_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c_{1}c_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the joint distribution over both concepts.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>DivDis</h3>

<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DivDis is a proper scoring rule if and only if the mix rate <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> equals the specific value <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that DivDis penalizes mutual information between concept distributions:</span></p>
<table id="A2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(p_{c_{1}},p_{c_{2}})=D_{\text{KL}}(p_{c_{1},c_{2}}||p_{c_{1}}\times p_{c_{2}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(p_{c_{1}},p_{c_{2}})=D_{\text{KL}}(p_{c_{1},c_{2}}||p_{c_{1}}\times p_{c_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">and note <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{KL}(q,p)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}(q,p)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. If DivDis is a proper scoring rule, then by definition <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> must attain this lower bound, i.e. <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(q_{c_{1}},q_{c_{2}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(q_{c_{1}},q_{c_{2}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The other direction also trivially holds. Thus concepts <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> mutual information under <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, recall that if two variables have 0 mutual information, they must be statistically independent, i.e.</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that the mix rate is given by <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mathbb{E}_{q}[\mathbf{1}_{C_{1}(x)\neq C_{2}(x)}]=q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}+q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mathbb{E}_{q}[\mathbf{1}_{C_{1}(x)\neq C_{2}(x)}]=q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}+q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Substituting in the above, we get</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}+q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{0,1}+q_{c_{1},c_{2}}^{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}=r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}=r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">Showing the other direction, assume that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r^{*}=q_{c_{1}}^{0}q_{c_{2}}^{1}+q_{c_{1}}^{1}q_{c_{2}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by the definition of mix rate and the marginal covariance decomposition, we must have zero covariance between <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the concepts are statistically independent, with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{c_{1},c_{2}}=q_{c_{1}}p_{c_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{c_{1},c_{2}}=q_{c_{1}}p_{c_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{MI}(q_{c_{1}},q_{c_{2}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{MI}(q_{c_{1}},q_{c_{2}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for evenly balanced marginal concept distributions <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{c_{i}}^{0}=p_{c_{i}}^{1}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{c_{i}}^{0}=p_{c_{i}}^{1}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Dataset Class Distributions ‣ ACE and Diverse Generalization viaSelective Disagreement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for computations
of the “optimal” mix rate <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> given the marginal concept distributions
for all settings of the realistic datasets used in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>D-BAT</h3>

<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D-BAT is a proper scoring rule if and only if the mix rate <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, recall that D-BAT penalizes the expected negative log-probability of disagreement between concept distributions on each instance:</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1},p_{2})=\mathbb{E}\left[-\log\left(p_{c_{1}|x}^{0}p_{c_{2}|x}^{1}+p_{c_{1}|x}^{1}p_{c_{2}|x}^{0}\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1},p_{2})=\mathbb{E}\left[-\log\left(p_{c_{1}|x}^{0}p_{c_{2}|x}^{1}+p_{c_{1}|x}^{1}p_{c_{2}|x}^{0}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the domain of the log argument is a probability and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly decreasing, this loss is minimized when the probability of disagreement is 1 for all instances, with <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1},p_{2})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(p_{1},p_{2})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper scoring rule, then <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{c_{1}|x}^{0}q_{c_{2}|x}^{1}+q_{c_{1}|x}^{1}q_{c_{2}|x}^{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{c_{1}|x}^{0}q_{c_{2}|x}^{1}+q_{c_{1}|x}^{1}q_{c_{2}|x}^{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. But if this equality holds, then <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{C_{1}(x)\neq C_{2}(x)}]=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_{C_{1}(x)\neq C_{2}(x)}]=r</annotation></semantics></math>. Going the other way, if <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> then the probability of disagreement is 1 on all inputs, so the expectation of the negative log is 0.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>ACE</h3>

<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ACE-TopK is a proper scoring rule if and only if each group mix rate lower bound does not exceed the true mix rate, i.e. <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall g^{\prime}\in G^{\prime}:\bar{r}_{g^{\prime}}\leq r_{g^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall g^{\prime}\in G^{\prime}:\bar{r}_{g^{\prime}}\leq r_{g^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">To avoid complications with batch sizing, we assume the inputs <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled uniformly from a discrete set <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. A simplified version of the ACE loss is given below:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACE}}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ACE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACE}}(p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[m_{i}^{(0,1)}\cdot-(\log{p_{c_{1}|x}^{0}}+\log{p_{c_{2}|x}^{1}})+m_{i}^{(1,0)}\cdot-(\log{p_{c_{1}|x}^{1}}+\log{p_{c_{2}|x}^{0}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[m_{i}^{(0,1)}\cdot-(\log{p_{c_{1}|x}^{0}}+\log{p_{c_{2}|x}^{1}})+m_{i}^{(1,0)}\cdot-(\log{p_{c_{1}|x}^{1}}+\log{p_{c_{2}|x}^{0}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{i}^{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{i}^{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow\begin{cases}1&amp;\mbox{ ... if }i\in\mbox{topk}(k_{\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{P}^{\mathbf{y}})\\
0&amp;\mbox{ ... otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> … if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>topk</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> … otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow\begin{cases}1&amp;\mbox{ ... if }i\in\mbox{topk}(k_{\mathbf{y}},\mathcal{P}^{\mathbf{y}})\\
0&amp;\mbox{ ... otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathbf{y}}=\lfloor N\cdot\bar{r}_{\mathbf{y}}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathbf{y}}=\lfloor N\cdot\bar{r}_{\mathbf{y}}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of probabilities each instance has the concept labels <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note the slight abuse of notation, replacing the group index <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with the corresponding concept values <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for clarity. This loss is bounded below by <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, with the bound obtained when the probabilities are 1 for all unmasked instances.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">First assume the true mix rate for each group is greater than the lower bound. Then <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathbf{y}}=\lfloor N\cdot\bar{r}_{\mathbf{y}}\rfloor\leq|\{x|\mathbf{c}(x_{j})=\mathbf{y}\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathbf{y}}=\lfloor N\cdot\bar{r}_{\mathbf{y}}\rfloor\leq|\{x|\mathbf{c}(x_{j})=\mathbf{y}\}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the true distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is able to perfectly sort instances, the top-k most likely instances to have concept labels <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> will actually have such labels, thus achieving 0 loss.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Going the other way, assume <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ACE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper scoring rule and that the lower bound does not hold, i.e. <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathbf{y}}&gt;|\{x|\mathbf{c}(x_{j})=\mathbf{y}\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathbf{y}}&gt;|\{x|\mathbf{c}(x_{j})=\mathbf{y}\}|</annotation></semantics></math> for disagreeing labels <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the top-k function </span><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">must</span><span id="A2.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"> select an instance <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}(x)\neq\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}(x)\neq\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> which will accrue non-zero loss under <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting our initial assumption.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Complete Correlation Datasets</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian Blob 2D Grid</span>: A modified version of the toy 2D grid dataset
introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>,
where instead of uniformly distributing data across quadrants,
we sample data points from 2D gaussian distributions centered in the middle of each quadrant,
with a standard deviation of 0.01, more closely resembling binary features
separated by low density regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FashionMNIST-MNIST</span>: A dataset of concatenated FashionMNIST
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> and MNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> images
introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>,
following the “dominos” format introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>
(see CIFAR-MNIST below).
The source distribution contains MNIST <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated with FashionMNIST tops,
and MNIST <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated with FashionMNIST bottoms.
The target dataset contains some instances of MNIST <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated to FashionMNIST bottoms,
and MNIST <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated to FashionMNIST tops.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIFAR-MNIST</span>: A dataset of concatenated CIFAR-10 and MNIST images
introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>.
The source distribution contains MNIST <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated with CIFAR cars,
and MNIST <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated with CIFAR trucks.
The target dataset contains some instances of MNIST <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated to CIFAR trucks,
and MNIST <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s concatenated to CIFAR cars.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Waterbirds-CC</span>: A modified version of Waterbirds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>
introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>
to evaluate complete spurious correlation. Waterbirds-CC contains only images of land birds on land
and waterbirds on water in the source distribution,
and some instances of land birds on water and waterbirds on land in the target distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CelebA-CC</span>: A modified version of the CelebA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>,
CelebA-CC contains only images of blond woman and dark-haired men on the source distribution,
but some instances of blond men and dark-haired woman on the target distribution
(where the ground truth feature is hair color and spurious feature is gender).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-NLI-CC-Binary</span>: A modified version of Multi-NLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>
Similar to Multi-NLI-CC introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>,
but groups entailment and neutral into a single label and filters out instances of
contradictions without negation from the source distribution. Thus for the source distribution,
all instances contain either entailment or neutral with no negation words, or
contradictions with negation words. The target distribution contains all four groups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Dataset Class Distributions</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Robustness to Mix Rate Variations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ACE and Diverse Generalization viaSelective Disagreement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, the three “realistic” datasets
used to evaluate diverse generalization methods (Waterbirds-CC, CelebA-CC, Multi-NLI-CC-Binary)
have class and group imbalances, which can impact the performance of methods and
our interpretation of the results. Below we provide the</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A4.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
